{
  "slug": "verify-preorder-serialization-of-a-binary-tree",
  "hints": [
    "Think of every non-null node as needing one existing child slot and creating two new child slots.",
    "A '#' consumes a slot but does not create any new slots.",
    "If available slots ever become negative, the preorder is impossible.",
    "After the last token, a valid serialization must have exactly zero available slots."
  ],
  "title": "Verify Preorder Serialization of a Binary Tree Problem",
  "content": {
    "notes": [
      "Each token is either a non-negative integer in the range [0, 100] or '#', separated by commas.",
      "A valid serialization uses exactly one available child slot for each token; a non-null node creates two new child slots."
    ],
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= preorder.length <= 10^4",
      "preorder consists of integers in the range [0, 100] and '#' separated by commas ','."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[\"9,3,4,#,#,1,#,#,2,#,6,#,#\"]",
        "output": "true",
        "explanation": "The sequence exactly describes the tree in preorder: every non-null node receives two child positions, and all positions are filled by the end.",
        "expectedOutput": "true"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[\"1,#\"]",
        "output": "false",
        "explanation": "The root still has one child position after reading 1,#, so the serialization ends too early.",
        "expectedOutput": "false"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[\"9,#,#,1\"]",
        "output": "false",
        "explanation": "After 9,#,# the tree is already complete, so the extra token 1 cannot be attached anywhere.",
        "expectedOutput": "false"
      }
    ],
    "problemStatement": "One way to serialize a binary tree is to use **preorder traversal**. When we encounter a non-null node, we record the node's value. If it is a null node, we record using a sentinel value such as `'#'`.\n\n![](https://assets.leetcode.com/uploads/2021/03/12/pre-tree.jpg)\n\nAs an example, the above binary tree can be serialized to the string `\"9,3,4,#,#,1,#,#,2,#,6,#,#\"`, where `'#'` represents a null node.\n\nWith a string of comma-separated values `preorder`, output `true` if it is a correct preorder traversal serialization of a binary tree.\n\nIt is **guaranteed** that each comma-separated value in the string must be either an integer or a character `'#'` representing null pointer.\n\nYou may assume that the input format is always valid.\n\n* As an example, it could never contain two consecutive commas, such as `\"1,,3\"`.\n\n**Note:**You are not allowed to reconstruct the tree.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: preorder = \"9,3,4,#,#,1,#,#,2,#,6,#,#\"\nOutput: true\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: preorder = \"1,#\"\nOutput: false\n```\n\n**Example 3:**\n\n```\nInput: preorder = \"9,#,#,1\"\nOutput: false\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n* `1 <= preorder.length <= 10^4`\n* `preorder` consist of integers in the range `[0, 100]` and `'#'` separated by commas `','`."
  },
